KNOWNBUG gcc16
main.cpp

^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
GCC 16 uses __allocated_obj (inheriting from __allocated_ptr)
as an RAII allocation guard. __allocate_guarded_obj returns
__allocated_obj via brace-init: return { __allocate_guarded(a) }.
CBMC's brace-init handling incorrectly uses aggregate
initialization instead of calling __allocated_obj's constructor
(__allocated_obj(__allocated_ptr&&)). The aggregate init tries
to assign __allocated_ptr to the base class's pointer member,
causing 'invalid implicit conversion'. The function body is
lost, symex treats it as nondeterministic, and the RAII guard's
conditional destructor causes path explosion.
Root cause: [dcl.init.list] p3 — brace-init for non-aggregate
class types should call constructors, not do aggregate init.
